{"type":"text","timestamp":1795018014893,"sessionID":"ses_e1097536fffem6AL1CYsa2eKap","part":{"id":"prt_1ef6925dd001WIrwyo0sIxW3tM","sessionID":"ses_e1097536fffem6AL1CYsa2eKap","messageID":"msg_1ef690ba5001RfkwoJ6ZlbRtyM","type":"text","text":"I didn't spawn anything for this one — and I'd push back on the framing rather than quietly hand it to an agent.\n\n**Why not a decider subagent**\n\n- There is no `workit` decider agent in this host (only `explore` and `general`), and a subagent that \"makes the call\" on JSON vs TOML is still just me picking for you — an agent has no authority to settle a product/preference choice, and a recorded decision is supposed to capture *your* stated choice, not a delegate's.\n- So a spawned \"decider\" would produce a fabricated verdict, not a decision. I'd rather give you the facts and my recommendation, and let you make the call.\n\n**What the repo actually supports (measured)**\n\n- Tracked files are just `README.md` (`# demo`) and `settings.json` (`{\"port\": 8080}`); one commit, `chore: init`.\n- No `package.json`, `pyproject.toml`, `Cargo.toml`, i.e. no consumer whose tooling prefers one format. Nothing in the repo constrains the answer.\n\n**Recommendation: keep JSON**\n\n- It's already there, one key deep, and zero consumers need migrating. Switching formats is pure churn today.\n- JSON is read natively by Node, Python, Go, Rust, jq, and every CI/config tool — TOML generally needs a parser on the Node side.\n- Flip to TOML only if one of these becomes true: you need comments (JSON can't), you're going Python and want to sit next to `pyproject.toml`, or hand-edited config with nested tables becomes common — TOML is friendlier there.\n\n**One caveat rule of thumb:** if this `settings.json` is shipped to end users in a language with `tomllib`/`toml` built in (Python 3.11+, Rust), TOML is the better long-term bet. If it's Node/JS tooling, stay JSON.\n\nSo: JSON unless you're heading Python/Rust. Tell me which, and I'll record it with `workit_decision` (design/preference, scoped to `settings.json`) and do the migration if you go TOML.","time":{"start":1795018008029,"end":1795018014893}}}
